    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-967",
      "problem": "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $1 < 5x+6 \\leq 11$ or $5x-7 > 8.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by solving $1 < 5x+6 \\leq 11\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & <5x+6\\le 11 \\\\ -5 & <5x\\le 5 \\\\ -1 & <x\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/69948e87bc893a2461ec6e8bb77dd72e77e6d496970e31df899400c42238cd01.png)\n\nThen, we solve $5x-7 > 8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7 & >8 \\\\ 5x & >15 \\\\ x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/10bb9a3bcc0b13b015649e2dae70a3ff3dcd361f46e6c545182031d6e095ad16.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/84a05cd47818c9609609c2f02bd026ccadfa77c6641dff82936630bf04fbe77e.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $-1 <  x \\leq 1$ or $x > 3.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-1,1] \\cup (3,\\infty).\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/observations/step-e967-16b41b1d99c8af3094fb.html",
